  {
   "id": "LTefnAdwFwAJ",
   "from": "Mike Collyer",
   "author": "aab55242ff4da6c6",
   "date": "2019-01-28T18:38:07Z",
   "subject": "Re: [geomorph-r-package] GPA in geomorph?",
   "parts": [
    [
     1,
     "<div style=\"word-wrap:break-word;line-break:after-white-space\">Hi Mohamed,<div><br></div><div>Good question!  The Q value gpagen returns is related to the tol value that the Morpho package allows one to vary.  Q is the statistic calculated after every iteration to compare to tol (which yes, is arbitrary) to determine if additional GPA permutations should be run.  Increasing the number of sliding landmarks will indeed affect the convergence (by making it more difficult unless the tol value is made larger).  We could possibly add this argument to gpagen, but we have taken a different approach with the max.iter.  The issue is if the tol value is too small and Q does not approach it, GPA iterations will run ad infinitum.  Therefore, we allow the user to specify when to stop GPA and by viewing Q, one can decide if they are happy with the convergence or whether to increase max.iter and try again.  There is a point where changing tol or max.iter does not improve the solution; i.e., Q might have a basement level it cannot pass through more permutations.</div><div><br></div><div>We made this choice because whether semi-landmarks are on curves or surfaces, and whether one uses bending energy or minimized Procrustes distance, the tol value, as a choice, is unintuitive and variable but the maximum number of iterations is clear, will not cause infinite loops, and is just more user-controlled.  We could hypothetically add both arguments but we worried initially that someone accidentally making the tol value too large would have a GPA that results in inferior convergence, as that argument would override the max.iter choice.  (We chose the safer route.)  </div><div><br></div><div>I could be swayed to reconsider if users really want to be able to change the tol value, though.</div><div><br></div><div>Cheers!</div><div>Mike<br><div><br><blockquote type=\"cite\"></blockquote></div></div></div>"
    ],
    [
     3,
